1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:15,997
On the evening of the twenty-eighth of July 1794, a cart rolled through the streets of Paris towards the guillotine. In it sat a slight man of thirty-six, his shattered jaw bound with a bandage.

2
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:26,761
Crowds lined the route, and many cheered. The prisoner was Maximilien Robespierre. Days earlier, he had been one of the most powerful men in France.

3
00:00:27,861 --> 00:00:39,424
During the Reign of Terror, around seventeen thousand people were officially sentenced to death in France. Many thousands more died in prison, or were killed without any trial.

4
00:00:40,524 --> 00:00:52,300
Yet this was a man who had once argued for abolishing the death penalty. This is how he became the face of the Terror, and why historians still argue about how much of it was his.

5
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:07,620
Maximilien Robespierre was born in Arras, in northern France, on the sixth of May 1758. His mother died when he was six, and his father soon abandoned the family.

6
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:18,192
Raised by relatives, he was a serious, studious boy, and won a scholarship to one of the best schools in Paris, the Lycée Louis-le-Grand.

7
00:01:19,292 --> 00:01:30,428
In 1775, chosen as one of the school's best pupils, he read out a Latin address in honour of the new king, Louis the Sixteenth.

8
00:01:31,528 --> 00:01:38,269
He went home to Arras and became a lawyer, with a reputation for taking on the cases of poor clients.

9
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:55,339
In 1789, with France bankrupt, the king summoned the Estates-General, an old assembly of clergy, nobles and commoners. Robespierre was elected to represent the commoners of his region.

10
00:01:56,439 --> 00:02:01,708
That summer, Parisians stormed the Bastille fortress. The Revolution had begun.

11
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:12,707
In the new National Assembly, and at the Jacobin Club, a political society in Paris, Robespierre became known as an uncompromising democrat.

12
00:02:13,807 --> 00:02:27,119
He argued for the vote for all men, not just the wealthy, and for equal rights for Jews and free people of colour. In 1791, he called for the death penalty to be abolished.

13
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:37,563
He lived modestly, took no bribes, and was nicknamed the Incorruptible. His admirers loved his purity. His enemies found it chilling.

14
00:02:38,663 --> 00:02:50,332
In 1792, he opposed going to war with Austria, warning that no one loves armed missionaries. He lost the argument, and France went to war.

15
00:02:52,532 --> 00:03:02,452
That August, crowds stormed the royal palace and the monarchy fell. In September, more than a thousand prisoners were murdered in the jails of Paris.

16
00:03:03,552 --> 00:03:15,477
Robespierre was elected to the new National Convention. When it put the king on trial, he argued there should be no trial at all. Louis must die, he said, so that the country may live.

17
00:03:16,577 --> 00:03:23,255
The king was guillotined on the twenty-first of January 1793.

18
00:03:25,455 --> 00:03:36,975
By the spring of 1793, France was at war with most of Europe, and rebellions had broken out in the west and in several major cities.

19
00:03:38,075 --> 00:03:48,784
In June, the Convention expelled the Girondins, the moderate faction that had rivalled Robespierre's Jacobins. Many of their leaders were later executed.

20
00:03:49,884 --> 00:04:05,287
In July 1793, Robespierre joined the Committee of Public Safety, a body of twelve men that became, in effect, the government of France. He was not a dictator, but he was its most famous voice.

21
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:17,437
Fastidious and austere, he still powdered his hair in the old style. He saw the Republic as a struggle of virtue against its enemies, and he saw enemies everywhere.

22
00:04:19,637 --> 00:04:31,115
That September, the Law of Suspects allowed the arrest of almost anyone thought hostile to the Revolution. Historians estimate that hundreds of thousands were imprisoned.

23
00:04:32,215 --> 00:04:42,839
In Paris, the Revolutionary Tribunal tried suspects in hearings that grew ever shorter. The former queen, Marie Antoinette, was executed in October.

24
00:04:43,939 --> 00:04:55,843
Robespierre defended it openly. Terror, he told the Convention, is nothing other than justice, prompt, severe and inflexible. It was, he said, an emanation of virtue.

25
00:04:56,943 --> 00:05:10,959
The worst killing was in the provinces. In the Vendée, in western France, historians estimate that the war against a royalist uprising left well over a hundred thousand people dead, many of them civilians.

26
00:05:13,159 --> 00:05:26,449
By early 1794, some revolutionaries wanted the Terror to slow down. Their leading figure was Georges Danton, a hero of 1792.

27
00:05:27,549 --> 00:05:39,240
The Committee struck first at the radicals around the journalist Jacques Hébert, and then at Danton's moderates. Among them was Camille Desmoulins, Robespierre's old school friend.

28
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:50,388
Danton and Desmoulins were guillotined in April 1794. Robespierre had been a witness at Desmoulins's wedding.

29
00:05:51,488 --> 00:06:05,440
In June, Robespierre presided over the Festival of the Supreme Being, a vast ceremony for a new civic religion he had championed. Some colleagues mocked it, and wondered if he saw himself as its high priest.

30
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:18,508
Two days later came the Law of Twenty-Two Prairial, backed by Robespierre. It removed the right to a defence lawyer, and left the tribunal only two verdicts: acquittal or death.

31
00:06:19,608 --> 00:06:27,309
In the seven weeks that followed, known as the Great Terror, well over a thousand people were guillotined in Paris alone.

32
00:06:29,509 --> 00:06:43,739
Meanwhile, the war had turned. In June 1794, French armies won a major victory at Fleurus. To many deputies, the emergency that had justified the Terror seemed to be over.

33
00:06:44,839 --> 00:06:56,721
On the twenty-sixth of July, he gave a long speech warning of traitors in the Convention, but refused to name them. Every deputy with something to fear assumed he meant them.

34
00:06:57,821 --> 00:07:05,011
The next day, the ninth of Thermidor in the revolutionary calendar, they shouted him down and voted for his arrest.

35
00:07:06,111 --> 00:07:14,004
Supporters freed him, and he took refuge in the city hall. In the early hours, troops loyal to the Convention stormed it.

36
00:07:15,104 --> 00:07:23,083
His jaw was shattered by a pistol shot. Whether he tried to kill himself, or was shot by a gendarme, is still debated.

37
00:07:24,183 --> 00:07:36,215
That evening, he was guillotined with twenty-one others, including his younger brother, Augustin, and his ally Saint-Just. More than eighty of his supporters followed over the next two days.

38
00:07:38,415 --> 00:07:48,975
Within weeks, the machinery of the Terror was dismantled. The men who had brought Robespierre down blamed him for all of it, including much that they had done themselves.

39
00:07:50,075 --> 00:07:59,867
The case against him is serious. He justified terror in public, backed the law that sped up the killing, and helped send former friends to their deaths.

40
00:08:00,967 --> 00:08:14,833
His defenders, including some modern historians, point out that he was one of twelve on the Committee, that France faced invasion and civil war, and that he believed sincerely in democracy and equality.

41
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:24,893
In France, the argument has never ended. A metro station in a Paris suburb bears his name, but no street in Paris does today.

42
00:08:25,993 --> 00:08:39,732
Robespierre's story is a warning about certainty. He wanted a republic of virtue, and came to believe that those who stood in its way deserved to die. In the end, the machine he defended consumed him too.
